\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Controlled image-based time-series reconstruction workflow. Solid arrows show the signal/image path from the triaxial window $x\!\in \!\mathbb  {R}^{N\times 3}$ through per-axis encoding ($\mathcal  {E}_m\!:\!\mathcal  {X}_m\!\to \!\mathbb  {R}^{N_m\times N_m}$, with $N_m\!=\!N$ for GASF/GADF/MTF and $N_m\!=\!N\!-\!1$ for RP) and method-tailored inversion ($\mathcal  {D}_m$), which returns a normalised reconstruction $\hat  {\tilde  {x}}^{(m)}\!\in \!\mathcal  {X}_m$. The final block is dual: the decoder output can be consumed directly in the normalised domain, or passed through the \emph  {optional} physical-scale recovery $\mathcal  {N}_m^{-1}$ to obtain $\hat  {x}$ in accelerometer units. Dashed arrows show auxiliary information recorded during encoding: the per-axis $(\min ,\max )$ calibration, used \emph  {only} when physical-scale recovery is requested, and the original window used as ground truth for visual, pointwise, and trend metrics. The RP decoder additionally consumes its own per-axis RP-matrix range $(R_{\min },R_{\max })$ --- needed even for the normalised reconstruction and distinct from the physical-scale $(\min ,\max )$ --- omitted from the arrows to keep the diagram legible.}}{3}{}\protected@file@percent }
